WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.390
El Salvador is a small country.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.230
In fact, it’s the smallest country in Central
America.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.910
But, that’s only geographically.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:21.300
The country has 6.5 million inhabitants, a
population that’s larger than Panama, Costa

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.100
Rica, Belize or Nicaragua.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.340
That’s a large population, especially when
you consider that emigration from the country

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.330
is common.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:35.281
According to various sources, some 3 to 4
million Salvadorans are living abroad, with

00:00:35.281 --> 00:00:37.090
many living in the US...

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.559
Shhh, don’t tell Trump!

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:45.059
Emigration is ever popular with thousands
of Salvadorans each year packing up their

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:52.469
suitcases and take a chance on a better future
somewhere else.

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:58.339
If this rate of emigration from El Salvador
maintains, there might soon be more Salvadorans

00:00:58.339 --> 00:01:01.609
living outside Salvador than living inside
it!

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:07.020
So now, you are probably wondering why so
many people are leaving El Salvador (or not,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.170
I mean we had to title this video something!).

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:15.110
But let have a look at why so many people
are leaving this country.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:21.440
Yes, it is true that El Salvador is a very
poor country, but economics is not the only

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.680
reason people are leaving.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.280
Now, before we get into details on what’s
causing this exodus, let me give you a quick

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.460
bit of information:
El Salvador is a tropical country where you

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.490
can find stunning beaches like these, as well
as magnificent nature reserves like this one,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.610
and amazing cultural remains.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:42.360
And in 2017 this country is celebrating.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.600
2017 is the 25th anniversary of the Peace
Agreements which put an end to the bitter

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.030
and cruel civil war which last more than 12
years and caused more than 70,000 deaths.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:02:00.370
The 
conflict was between the national armed forces

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:05.320
and the so-called Farabundo Martí National
Liberation Front, a left-wing paramilitary

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.030
guerrilla group which counted on the support
of the Cuban Government.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.870
Interestingly, after the peace agreements,
this guerrilla group became a political party

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.670
and today they have the Presidency of the
Republic…

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:21.200
Quite the remarkable change.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:30.370
Now, while this 25th anniversary should be
reason for celebration, it is hard to celebrate

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:35.500
when you look at the current situation that
El Salvador finds itself in.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:42.300
THE VIOLENCE
The 11th January 2017, was a normal Wednesday

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.720
when something extraordinary happened: absolutely
NOTHING…

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.160
So, let me explain, that day ended with a
death toll of zero in El Salvador.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.040
And yes, that non-event was actually front-page
news.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.470
The reason for this being newsworthy is that
El Salvador has become one of the most dangerous

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.270
countries in the world.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:07.090
To give you an idea of how dire the situation
is:

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.400
In 2015 6,670 murders were registered.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:15.170
That is to say there were 105 murders for
every 100,000 inhabitants.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:21.650
This is the highest rate any country in the
world which is not at war.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.730
This number is truly terrifying.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:32.250
For comparison, it would be as if there were
70,000 murders in the UK a year...

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.300
The real figure for the UK?

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.030
A touch more than 500.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.230
That’s 140 TIMES MORE MURDER!

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.440
In 2016, the number was a bit lower, but still
almost 4000 murders were registered.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.440
It’s madness.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.020
There have been wars where less people died!

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.600
Oh, and believe me, murder is far from the
only serious crime going down in El Salvador:

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.910
kidnapping, extortion, thefts…

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.380
all worryingly common.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.150
So, who's up for a holiday to El Salvador?!

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:00.150
No?

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.150
No-one?!

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:06.210
Considering all of this it’s probably not
so surprising that according to recent surveys

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:11.310
conducted of the population that nearly half
of them want to leave the country!

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.340
It’s clear to see that violence has consequences.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.630
And this is not just fear of a crime being
committed directly against you, but it also

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.280
affects the standard of living of the El Salvadorians.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.180
According to the Salvadoran Central Bank,
violence costs around $4 billion every year.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.540
And, you know what?

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.250
That’s not really very suprising.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.350
After all, who really wants to invest his
money in a country where you can be killed

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.350
any time.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.550
Don't believe me?

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:41.040
“El Salvador's foreign investment is the
lowest in of all Central America”

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.530
Even regular people are at risk in El Salvador.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:47.770
In fact the statistics show that it is more
dangerous to be a bus driver than it is to

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.190
be a police officer in the country.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:57.600
&nbsp;In the last 5 years, street gangs have killed
almost 700 bus drivers.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.840
VIOLENCE IN THE REGION
It is known globally that Latin America has

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.430
a serious problem with violence and crime.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.970
According to a report from the British consulting
firm Verisk Maplecroft, Latin America is the

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.560
region where you have the highest risk of
having a violent crime committed against you.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:19.470
Indeed, 6 of the world's 10 most violent countries
are in this region.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:29.660
Incredibly, 1 in 3 homicides that occur every
year globally take place in Latin America.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:35.560
Even in this dangerous environment, the northern
triangle of Central America takes the biscuit.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:40.820
This area is formed of El Salvador, Honduras
and Guatemala, and it is, by far, the most

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.020
dangerous area in the continent.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:47.760
In fact it is the most dangerous region of
the world that is not currently a war zone.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.760
In these countries, which are relatively small,
200,000 people have been murdered since 2004.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:06:01.310
When you think about the reality of these
numbers, it’s understandable why people

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:03.940
want to leave a country where you can’t
walk the streets at night safely, or wear

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:10.060
a watch or necklace, or start a business without
having to pay some money to a criminal organisation.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:16.320
So the question must now be: Why is there
quite so much violence?

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:28.040
DRUG-TRAFFICKING AND “MARAS”
However, these three countries in Central

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.570
America have not always been such dangerous
places.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:34.960
They could even have been considered relatively
peaceful until about 20 years ago - at least

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.240
at the individual level.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.590
But this all started to change by the end
of the 80s and 90s for two reasons: drug-trafficking

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.910
and Maras.

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:52.509
First: drug-trafficking is a serious problem
in the region.

00:06:52.509 --> 00:06:57.060
Every year, 22 million Americans use drugs
and most of them come from South America.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:05.620
The drug cartels are making an absolute fortune
from this.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:14.440
Obviously, this isn't something new, but this
activity has evolved over the years, and has

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:17.970
continually found ways to operate despite
pressure from law enforcement.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:23.130
The persecution of the famous Colombian drug
cartels led the the route drugs take to the

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:29.560
US shifting from: Colombia-Caribbean Islands-Florida,
to a new route going through Central America,

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:31.110
and this is our first big problem.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:35.400
Central America has become a sort of sanctuary
for the new Mexican drug cartels.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.300
From Central America they can organize their
businesses, hide drugs and take refuge from

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.400
justice whenever needed.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.910
So you’re probably starting to get the idea.

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:49.830
Experts have calculated that 80% of the drugs
used in the US goes through Honduras.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.810
And this has lead to an extraordinary amount
of violence in the region.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.120
Now to our second major problem.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.120
The “Maras”.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.860
For those of you who don't know them, the
“Maras” are street gangs that engage in

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.419
all kinds of criminal activities like theft,
murder, or extortion.

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:06.229
Their members are usually young people and
their organization is relatively horizontal,

00:08:06.229 --> 00:08:08.880
meaning they don’t have much of an organised
hierarchy.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.979
They organize in criminal cells known a “clicas”,
which work quite independently from one another.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:17.530
Pay attention here because it is estimated
that in these three countries alone, the “maras”

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.210
have around 70,000 to 100,000 members.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:25.539
The most famous of these organizations are
“Salvatrucha” and “Barrio 18”, and

00:08:25.539 --> 00:08:30.000
both usually fight against each other over
the control of different territories.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.450
THE FIGHT AGAINST VIOLENCE
The situation is complex.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.620
Many strategies have been used to try and
solve the problem and they have all failed.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:44.180
However, two initiatives have been put into
practice lately in order to change the reality

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:47.260
of these countries and achieve safety for
the citizens.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.770
The first is known as “Fuerza de Tarea Trinacional”
(the Trinational Task Force): This is a security

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:55.959
apparatus formed by police officers, intelligence
experts, and special units of the three countries

00:08:55.959 --> 00:08:57.370
we have mentioned.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:01.459
This group aims to fight the street gangs,
drug-trafficking and organized crime regardless

00:09:01.459 --> 00:09:02.459
of borders.

00:09:02.459 --> 00:09:06.440
Also, the group receives the support, training,
and finance from the United States.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:12.150
The idea is clear: as Juan Orlando Hernandez,
the President of Honduras, likes to say:

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:17.110
“We are going to give them a taste of their
own medicine".

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:21.040
The second initiative is linked to the first
and it is known as the "Alliance for Prosperity"

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:26.020
and it was launched by the Obama Administration
and it expects to improve the living conditions

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.350
and the competitiveness of these countries.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:33.140
Its main goal is to create jobs and essential
infrastructure in order to reverse the terrible

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:34.420
current situation.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:41.880
To start, Washington has already committed
to spend $750 million to support these initiatives.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.370
But, is there hope?

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.010
Well, there sort of has to be.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:50.940
Mara Salvatrucha has already communicated
they are willing to negotiate their own dismantling.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:54.050
Only time will tell if any of this is successful.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:58.810
So that’s our view on the region, but what
do you think?

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:02.100
Will these countries get rid of criminals?

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.140
Will the plan set by Washington succeed?

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:10.390
I´m looking forward to your answers and with
the growth of this channel we can’t respond

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:13.180
to them all, but know that we do read them!

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:18.260
As always, hit like if you enjoyed the video
and don’t forget to subscribe for new videos

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:20.130
every Monday and Thursday.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.790
And as always, I’ll see you next time.

